reference:
PROBLEMS FIXED:
  1. Guard the actual nullable certificate date before ordered comparisons. When disposed is null the original > test throws; the guarded expression yields false.
  2. A completed disposal must be at or before inspection, not at or after it. A disposal on Jan 2 with inspection Jan 3 must qualify.
  3. Copy disposed from the certificate, preserving null, instead of substituting the inspection date. For those dates the old output is Jan 3 instead of Jan 2.

Left unchanged: `date filingOrigin;` already reads as the epoch. Python has no equivalent bare typed declaration that binds a value: reading an unbound local raises UnboundLocalError, while the nullable certificate field really can be null here.

CORRECTED CODE:
```ctl
//#CTL2
function integer transform() {
    date filingOrigin;
    $out.0.retired = !isnull($in.1.disposed) && $in.1.disposed > filingOrigin && $in.1.disposed <= $in.0.inspection;
    $out.0.asset = $in.0.asset;
    $out.0.disposed = $in.1.disposed;
    return ALL;
}
```

They differ. CTL2 supplies "" to the local string, so its first length is 0; an unbound Python local name would raise an error. A nullable output field with no default that is never assigned stays null because it follows record metadata, not the local declaration rule.

```ctl
//#CTL2
function integer transform() {
    string scratchNote;
    $out.0.local_length = length(scratchNote);
    return ALL;
}
```

